<div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker reveal">Features</p>
        <h1 class="reveal d2">Sign-in, users and permissions, <em>in one package</em>.</h1>
        <div class="page-head-grid">
            <p class="lede reveal d3">Booster Auth runs authentication, user management and authorization as a single product on your own infrastructure.
                Every feature on this page is in every plan; plans differ only by their limits.</p>
            <div class="cta-row reveal d3">
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/en/download">Download and try <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/en/docs/concepts">Core concepts</a>
            </div>
        </div>
        <dl class="ledger">
            <div><dt>Protocol</dt><dd>OIDC, OAuth 2.0, PKCE S256</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Sign-in</dt><dd>Password, passwordless codes, 2FA, LDAP / AD</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Authorization</dt><dd>Roles, scopes and an OpenFGA schema</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Deployment</dt><dd>Docker Compose, hardened services</dd></div>
        </dl>
    </div>
</div>

<section id="sign-in" aria-labelledby="h-signin">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">Authentication</p>
                <h2 id="h-signin">A standard protocol, sign-in tuned per pool.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Your applications speak plain OpenID Connect. Sign-in methods, password rules and session lifetimes are set separately for every user pool.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="caps">
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>OpenID Connect</h3><p class="lab">OAuth 2.0 · RS256</p></div>
                <p>One issuer for web, SPA, mobile and server applications: <code>https://auth.company.com/idp</code>, with standard discovery and JWKS.</p>
                <ul><li>Authorization Code + PKCE <span>(S256 required)</span></li><li>Refresh tokens</li><li>Client credentials <span>(service accounts)</span></li><li>Token exchange</li><li>No implicit flow</li><li>RS256-signed tokens</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Sign-in methods</h3><p class="lab">Per pool</p></div>
                <p>The user identifier can be an e-mail, a username or a phone number. Sign-in and sign-up screens come in English and Turkish.</p>
                <ul><li>Password sign-in</li><li>Passwordless e-mail codes</li><li>Passwordless SMS codes</li><li>6-digit codes, void after 5 wrong tries</li><li>Password reset</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Two-factor</h3><p class="lab">E-mail · SMS</p></div>
                <p>A second step after password sign-in with a code sent by e-mail or SMS. It can be made mandatory per pool.</p>
                <ul><li>E-mail code</li><li>SMS code</li><li>Required per pool</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Passwords and lockout</h3><p class="lab">Policy</p></div>
                <p>Passwords are hashed with peppered Argon2. Rules are set per pool.</p>
                <ul><li>Minimum length</li><li>Upper / lower case, digits, symbols</li><li>Expiry</li><li>Password history</li><li>Lockout after failed attempts</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Sessions and consent</h3><p class="lab">Lifetimes · revocation</p></div>
                <p>Access tokens last 15 minutes and refresh tokens 14 days by default; change both in the pool's session policy.</p>
                <ul><li>Idle timeout</li><li>Consent screen per application</li><li>List and revoke sessions</li><li>List and revoke consents</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>External providers</h3><p class="lab">Federation</p></div>
                <p>Users sign in with an account they already have; the account can be created on first sign-in, and the identity record stays with you.</p>
                <ul><li>Google</li><li>Microsoft</li><li>Apple</li><li>LinkedIn</li><li>Facebook</li><li>GitHub</li><li>X</li><li>Any OpenID Connect provider</li><li>SAML</li></ul>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="ldap" class="band-2" aria-labelledby="h-ldap">
    <div class="wrap console-grid" style="grid-template-columns:minmax(0,5fr) minmax(0,7fr)">
        <div class="reveal">
            <p class="kicker">LDAP / Active Directory</p>
            <h2 id="h-ldap">Employees sign in with their corporate accounts.</h2>
            <p class="prose-2" style="margin-top:20px">No extra button to find: users sign in on the normal form with their directory username and password.
                Passwords stay in the directory; Booster Auth never stores them.</p>
            <ul class="notes">
                <li><b>Configured per pool</b>On the Identity Providers screen: server, LDAPS or StartTLS, service account bind, search base, filter and attribute mapping.</li>
                <li><b>Test before you save</b>The “Test connection” button confirms the settings work.</li>
                <li><b>Accounts appear by themselves</b>The account is created on first sign-in; name and e-mail are refreshed from the directory every time.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal d2"><partial name="~/Views/Site/_LdapMock.cshtml" /></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="users" aria-labelledby="h-users">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">User management</p>
                <h2 id="h-users">Every audience gets its own pool and its own rules.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>Under a tenant you create isolated user pools. Users, sessions and permissions never mix between pools; the same e-mail can be two separate accounts in two pools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="caps">
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Tenants and pools</h3><p class="lab">Tenant · pool</p></div>
                <p>Each pool has its own sign-in, password, MFA, session, registration and redirect policy. Think “Customers” and “Employees”.</p>
                <ul><li>Isolated pools</li><li>Policy per pool</li><li>Default roles and scopes</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Sign-up and invitations</h3><p class="lab">Open · invite-only</p></div>
                <p>Open self sign-up or invitation-only membership. Invitation links are single-use and valid for one day.</p>
                <ul><li>Add one by one with an e-mail invitation</li><li>Self sign-up</li><li>E-mail / phone verification</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>CSV import</h3><p class="lab">Up to 1000 rows</p></div>
                <p>Move an existing user list in one go and get a row-by-row result report.</p>
                <ul><li>English and Turkish column names</li><li>Comma or semicolon</li><li>UTF-8, up to 1 MB</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Service accounts</h3><p class="lab">Machine to machine</p></div>
                <p>Services that run without a human get tokens with a key and secret; the secret is shown only once.</p>
                <ul><li>Client credentials</li><li>Expiry</li><li>Secret rotation</li><li>Suspension</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Redirect policy</h3><p class="lab">Redirect URIs</p></div>
                <p>Redirect URIs must match exactly. Optionally, a pool's applications can only register URIs on domains you allow.</p>
                <ul><li>Exact matching</li><li>Per-pool domain allow-list</li><li>Test and mobile scheme options</li></ul>
            </article>
            <article class="cap reveal">
                <div><h3>Branded sign-in</h3><p class="lab">Per application</p></div>
                <p>Each application shows its own logo on the sign-in screen, and the consent screen is switched on or off per application.</p>
                <ul><li>Application logo</li><li>Consent screen</li><li>English / Turkish</li></ul>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="authorization" class="band" aria-labelledby="h-authz">
    <div class="wrap split">
        <div class="reveal">
            <p class="kicker">Authorization</p>
            <h2 id="h-authz">Start with roles, grow into relationships.</h2>
            <p class="prose-2" style="margin-top:20px">Simple applications are happy with the <code>role</code> and <code>scope</code> values in the token. When you need
                record-level rules, the OpenFGA-based authorization schema takes over: define resource types, the links between them and permissions, then
                ask from your application.</p>
            <ul class="terms">
                <li><b>Schema</b><span>Versioned (draft / live). Start from the Owner / Editor / Viewer template.</span></li>
                <li><b>API</b><span>Single and batch checks; lookups by user or by object; adding and removing relations.</span></li>
                <li><b>Console</b><span>A Relations screen and an access check to try out a user's permissions.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal d2">
            <div class="code"><span class="lbl">POST /authz/check</span><pre>{
  "isolationId": "&lt;pool id&gt;",
  "user": "user:&lt;user sub&gt;",
  "relation": "editor",
  "object": "document:order-2026-001"
}</pre></div>
            <div class="code"><span class="lbl">Response</span><pre>{ "data": { "allowed": true } }</pre></div>
            <p class="muted" style="font-size:15px">Details: <a class="text-link" href="/en/docs/authorization">the authorization checks guide</a>.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="security" aria-labelledby="h-sec">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec-head reveal">
            <div>
                <p class="kicker">Security and operations</p>
                <h2 id="h-sec">On your infrastructure, hardened from the start.</h2>
            </div>
            <p>One Docker Compose package with PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka and OpenFGA included. The setup script generates every password and key, and services start with restricted privileges.</p>
        </div>
        <dl class="spec reveal">
            <div><dt>Hardened services<small>read_only · non-root · cap_drop</small></dt>
                <dd>Read-only filesystems, non-root users, dropped Linux capabilities and <code>no-new-privileges</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Small network surface<small>HTTPS · console</small></dt>
                <dd>Only the HTTPS gateway and the admin console are exposed; infrastructure services open no ports.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Keys stay with you<small>.env · Argon2</small></dt>
                <dd>Passwords are hashed with peppered Argon2; sensitive data is encrypted with keys that exist only in your <code>.env</code> file. The system
                    will not start while any value is still <code>CHANGE_ME</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>HTTPS<small>local CA</small></dt>
                <dd>A local CA and certificate are generated on first start; use your own certificate or reverse proxy whenever you like.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Offline licensing<small>BSTR1</small></dt>
                <dd>Paste the license key into the console with no restart. The product never contacts a license server; usage and limits are shown in the console.</dd></div>
        </dl>
    </div>
</section>

